---
args: { input: CaseInput }
returns: RunProposal
---
1. Bind `goal = await frameRequest(input)` from the request and `policy = await selectPolicy(input)` from supplied authority candidates. Preserve unresolved precedence instead of guessing which source governs.
2. Bind `work = await formTaskGraph(goal)` and `evidencePlan = await planEvidence(input, work)` so each operation has explicit prerequisites and a searched source universe.
3. Run the stages in their dependency order. The first stage receives only `input.domainRecords.firstStage`; every later stage receives the preceding function's actual accepted records. Bind each full result separately.
const recoverBehaviorResult = await recoverBehavior(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan);
const modelPartitionsResult = await modelPartitions(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, recoverBehaviorResult.accepted);
const constructTestsResult = await constructTests(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, modelPartitionsResult.accepted);
const interpretFailuresResult = await interpretFailures(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, constructTestsResult.accepted);
const qualifySuiteResult = await qualifySuite(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, interpretFailuresResult.accepted);
4. Bind `stageSet: CaseStageSet = { recoverBehavior: recoverBehaviorResult, modelPartitions: modelPartitionsResult, constructTests: constructTestsResult, interpretFailures: interpretFailuresResult, qualifySuite: qualifySuiteResult }` and `collection: CollectionFinding = await reconcileCollection(stageSet)`. Bind `allQuestions: string[] = [...recoverBehaviorResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...modelPartitionsResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...constructTestsResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...interpretFailuresResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...qualifySuiteResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...collection.coverageGaps]` and `questions: QuestionBatch = await resolveQuestions(allQuestions, input)`.
5. Bind `invalidation = await assessDelta(input)`, `review = await prepareReview(collection)`, and `view = await composeView(review, input)`; pass each child its actual declared inputs and retain the returned object.
6. Bind `allChecks: CheckResult[] = [...recoverBehaviorResult.checks, ...modelPartitionsResult.checks, ...constructTestsResult.checks, ...interpretFailuresResult.checks, ...qualifySuiteResult.checks]`. Bind `hasGateFailure = allChecks.some(check => check.verdict !== "pass")` and `hasBlocker = input.authorityStatus !== "authorized" || questions.questions.length > 0 || collection.coverageGaps.length > 0`. Bind `disposition: "proposed" | "blocked" | "needs-review" = hasBlocker ? "blocked" : hasGateFailure ? "needs-review" : "proposed"`. Bind `rationale: string[] = allChecks.map(check => `${check.check}: ${check.verdict}; evidence ${check.evidenceIds.join(",")}`)`.
7. Operational detail 1 — A flaky test fails only under load: preserve timing evidence and avoid hiding it through unlimited retries. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (preserve timing evidence and avoid hiding it through unlimited retries.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralRequirement; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralRequirement, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
8. Operational detail 2 — A requirement changes after a test is approved: invalidate affected assertions explicitly. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (invalidate affected assertions explicitly.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralRequirement; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralRequirement, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
9. Operational detail 3 — A test uses production credentials: block execution before sandbox launch. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (block execution before sandbox launch.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralRequirement; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralRequirement, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
10. Operational detail 4 — A generated fixture contains personal data: replace it with approved synthetic records. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (replace it with approved synthetic records.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralRequirement; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralRequirement, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
11. Operational detail 5 — A patch makes tests pass by deleting an assertion: require independent coverage review. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (require independent coverage review.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralRequirement; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralRequirement, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
12. Initialize `revisions: RevisionProposal[] = []`. If any gate is not pass, bind `revision = await proposeRevision(the failed check names, the smallest affected stage name)` and add that returned proposal to revisions. Bind and return `result: RunProposal = { caseId: input.caseId, goal, policy, work, evidencePlan, recoverBehavior: recoverBehaviorResult, modelPartitions: modelPartitionsResult, constructTests: constructTestsResult, interpretFailures: interpretFailuresResult, qualifySuite: qualifySuiteResult, collection, questions, invalidation, review, view, revisions, disposition, rationale }`. Proposed actions remain proposals. The host owns authorization, persistence, effect dispatch and verification.
